WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
Is this is just our social gang?

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.480
And it's just us? I think that's a problem.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.580
Hi, I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and I am

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.540
talking with Jeff Tucker. He is the

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.250
Director of Content at the Foundation

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.980
for Economic Education—which I think is

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.500
the oldest—longest-lived

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.020
libertarian organization. Crazy. Look it

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.670
was founded in 1946.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.320
1946. Which we don't know

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.099
anything about that.

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.990
Yeah. But we we actually know from the

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.460
history books what was going on. Price

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.529
controls—you know—heavily regulated

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.870
industry. It was a wartime economy and a lot

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.190
of people at the time thought

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.079
that liberalism—oh, liberalism— was

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:39.840
dead and gone. But a handful of people—you

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.879
know—including Lawrence Fertig, Leonard Read—

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:44.219
you know it was just really a

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:46.170
small, small group. And wasn't like

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.090
Ludwig von Mises always hanging out out there?

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.860
So that's an interesting thing because he

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.770
came over 1940 and didn't really have a

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.410
home, anywhere really. He wanted to stay

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.489
in New York so he didn't have a

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:59.250
university position. Well FEE brought him on board.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.600
And made him a kind of a house

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.730
intellectual. You know Henry Hazlitt

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:03.840
at the time who wrote

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.840
Economics in One Lesson in 1946 also. And so

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.400
when I went to work for FEE I went back

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.590
because I wanted to do something about

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.350
reviving it's editorial mission.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.729
I didn't want to do the same thing

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:16.170
everybody else was doing. I wanted to do

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.390
something different. And I wanted to

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.000
figure out maybe there's

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.020
something we're missing.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.939
So I went back and looked at the early

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:25.680
writings of all these guys from the

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.060
1940s and I tell you

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.680
I found something really interesting and

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.200
it might be of interest to you. There weren't a lot

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.570
of what we call libertarians around at the time.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.610
They didn't have any—they didn't

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.340
have like we have a movement, you know?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.950
So that had posed a special editorial burden

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.820
on them. They had to speak in a way to

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.549
everybody or to anyone who would listen.

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:50.189
And I think that affected the way they

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:52.590
thought and the way they wrote. So they

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.240
had to like every piece had to sort of

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.120
make sense for anybody who happened to pick it up.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.840
So there—in that they're showing

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.350
their math. They're making their

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.420
arguments clear. They're not assuming a

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.250
lot of foreknowledge. That's right.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.200
And they're not using

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.420
a specialized language, right? So

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.459
they know better than to—and it's

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:11.780
very different from us today.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.700
But they spoke to a general—

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.100
a general audience in the values of the time.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.670
Trying to make a practical case

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.840
for human liberty.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.760
In the early stages of FEE publications did they link

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.160
economics to that broader issue of

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.360
human liberty? There was always a moral concern and

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.769
it's interesting because I saw a

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:33.470
correspondence between Ayn Rand and

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.030
Leonard Read. And she really wanted the

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.160
organization to be called something like—

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.989
I don't know— the Institute for the

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:41.030
Defense of Radical Capitalism or something like that.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.190
And Leonard was—you know

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.019
he came out of industry.

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:46.579
He came out of commerce and he understood

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:50.000
sales and marketing. And he knew that if

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.230
he called it the Foundation for Economic

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.269
Education—something very plain and

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:55.010
innocuous—that they could

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.000
push radical ideas in a mainstream framework.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.299
And wasn't one of their early

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:01.670
publications —I may be getting this wrong—

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:02.810
but it was something with

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.000
Milton Friedman and Stigler talking about

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.829
rent control? Yes. And that was the

00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:09.019
piece that drew Murray Rothbard my

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:11.750
old friend in an exciting

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.790
relationship with the ideas of liberty.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.709
And they did this with many

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:18.319
people. Now the height of the

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:20.720
Friedman circulation was

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.840
achieved in the 1960s with fifty thousand.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.340
Which, I mean, Reason gets that every day.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.579
And we do now. So it was a small

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:30.170
market —relatively small market. But what I

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.359
think is interesting is that they had a

00:03:32.359 --> 00:03:34.459
big job and they had really nowhere

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.900
to begin but just reaching mainstream people.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.980
And here's the thing. I think we

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.090
have something to learn from that.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.010
Because as we've become a movement—

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.720
that's very exciting. We love the movement and we

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.489
talk with libertarians. We come to libertarian events.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:48.049
We're at Students for

00:03:48.049 --> 00:03:50.329
Liberty where they've got about 1,700 kids

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.310
from a hundred countries. And we all—

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.889
we're all in the same Facebook groups and we

00:03:54.889 --> 00:03:57.200
follow each other on Twitter.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.519
And that's good.

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:00.319
Nothing bad about that. But it does give

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:02.030
rise to—I think—certain temptations to

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.849
speak in our own vernacular or our own

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:06.889
really high liturgical language with

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:08.389
each other. Then normal people

00:04:08.389 --> 00:04:09.080
can't understand.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.900
And it also tempts us to sort of dig ever

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.380
deeper into sectarian battles—

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.150
and here's the other thing—it's a matter of how we think

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.170
of ourselves.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.160
Are we a chosen, marginalized

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.710
group that's going to be

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.330
forever hanging around together?

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.960
Is this just our social gang?

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.060
And it's just us? I think that's a problem.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.800
So what are some of the articles that

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.900
you do that you try to reach a broader

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:37.070
general audience. So I don't know

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.720
if you're ever tempted to do this as an editor,

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:39.980
but I've never done this before. I've never tried this before.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.540
But I try to classify every article

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.700
as either in-reach or outreach.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.010
And by that I mean are we

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.530
cultivating the libertarian spirit

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.380
among the already convinced? Or am I creating

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.170
a piece of content that you as a

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.660
libertarian might be willing to share on

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.920
your Facebook account, even though your

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.380
pastor or your mother, your friends from high school are on it

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.390
because you found it compelling enough.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.190
And what I've learned from the metrics

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.470
is that this is the challenge. To create

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:09.160
content that gets the second, third, and fourth share.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.520
What's like the most

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.180
recent piece you did that was successful in those terms?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.180
Well you mentioned my review of "The Young Pope" and we—

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.310
So"The Young Pope" is the HBO show with

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.590
Jude Law and—were you raised

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.050
Catholic by any chance? Well— [CROSSTALK]

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.880
but you're familiar? I've had a big

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.410
relationship with Catholicism [CROSSTALK]

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.260
So I was raised Catholic and I

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.510
actually I love it. I'm a

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:35.450
prolapsed Catholic at this point. I'm like

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.490
totally—it's collapsed and it's not coming

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.350
back.  You'll never be anything but

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.120
ex-Catholic, Nick. You can't get away from it.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.160
But I mean I love the show. But talk a

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.630
little bit about that piece because that

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:47.100
seemed to be doing something that you're talking about.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.560
That's the kind of thing we. We try to review all the new stuff on Netflix.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.460
And we try to write regular reviews. In other words

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.510
I'm looking for a libertarian spin

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.060
on things. I want really regular reviews

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.220
that I as a libertarian am

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.630
going to write because I see things

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.640
differently than other people. And I hope to

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.380
add value to it you know?

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.880
One of the things we used to talk about

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.610
at Reason and over the years

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.930
you come up with different things.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.630
But we wanted to be not—we didn't want

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.910
to just be a magazine for libertarians.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:16.070
We wanted to be a magazine by libertarians

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.830
because your worldview would fill out.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.960
[CROSSTALK] I think that's a good way to

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.550
think about it. That's a good way to think about it.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:23.990
Reason does a good job of this. What was

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.880
your insight for "The Young Pope"?

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.340
Because I thought it was kind of clever.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.930
I think the whole thing was structured

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.800
in a way to respond to the rise of

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.770
a kind of reactionary right. In Europe

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.960
and in the United States. And this show is very

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.330
popular in Italy. So the young pope

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:42.979
shows up. He had a regular career. He had

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:44.810
the perfect bishop career. Just the right

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.570
training and never revealed much about

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.360
himself. Just very pastoral as they say.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:51.620
They knew he was a conservative because

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.509
of course look at his teachers and look

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:54.680
at his temperament and that sort of thing.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.020
What they didn't know is that he had

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.780
a hard agenda and really a kind of

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.320
revanchist agenda to recapture what

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.599
would have been lost. And he really does

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:06.860
adopt all these forms that are

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.169
essentially pre-Vatican too. And

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.639
there are moments in the thing that just had

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:13.280
me just cracking up. He delayed

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.310
weeks his opening lecture to the College of

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.050
Cardinals and by

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.210
the final thing the thing comes along

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.120
he's carried in on a chair and wearing

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.949
the most outrageous liturgical gear

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:27.590
seeming to revive the temporal power.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.150
Oh, and he brings back the triple crown

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.550
which Paul VI gave away.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.110
He gets it shipped from Washington.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.690
And then at the same time he is

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.580
hyper-conservative or traditional but he

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.050
breaks all the forms.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.880
He even starts smoking again in

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.979
the Vatican which is— And also I

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:44.630
think it was very interesting for me to

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.789
follow it because his commitment was

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:48.289
entirely intellectual but it wasn't

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:50.630
organically connected with the real

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.490
experience of the church now.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.560
And what he finds

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.770
is that there's no going back. Which is

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.180
not to say that it's inevitable,

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.759
we're going to do forever and it tended in

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:03.620
Francis direction. The point is that

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:04.789
whatever you're going to build you have

00:08:04.789 --> 00:08:07.610
to build from what actually really exists.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.740
Well and you also talked about it how

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.720
"The Young Pope"— and again this is a show

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.729
that was filmed in Italy a year or a year

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:16.309
and a half ago. So it's pre-Trump but he's

00:08:16.309 --> 00:08:19.009
also kind of the perfect—one way to talk

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:22.039
about Trump because he has a very set—

00:08:22.039 --> 00:08:23.960
strong set of views that he is going to

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.749
force on people. Whether he likes it or not.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:30.020
[CROSSTALK] I read reviews

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.879
of things partly because I'm dodging my

00:08:31.879 --> 00:08:34.159
own deadlines but it's also like I had a

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:36.079
professor once who said, "Look, any

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:38.750
criticism is good if it makes the

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.430
thing more interesting and I

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:42.320
thought that your Young Pope piece

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.120
worked really well. Now had you seen it?

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.430
Yeah. And you still liked my review?

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:47.870
Oh, totally. It gave you some

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.459
insight. No and it—

00:08:49.459 --> 00:08:51.860
it also made me think about Trump

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.380
who you know I'm not a fan of

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.839
Trump. One of the things though I do find

00:08:56.839 --> 00:08:59.149
attractive about him is his willingness

00:08:59.149 --> 00:09:01.430
to break with decorum and just be like

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.500
shut the fuck up like we're doing it my

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.350
way now. And he will say this is the way

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.709
it should always be done. And that is—it's

00:09:09.709 --> 00:09:12.110
chaos inducing. I think it is not principled—

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:15.139
And maybe we need that. [CROSSTALK] It can

00:09:15.139 --> 00:09:17.329
make an opening for more radical politics I hope.

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:19.760
I hope that that's true. You know on the

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.100
other side, we had a review of this

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.660
movie about Queen Elizabeth II—"The Crown."

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:28.399
And now there you have an opposite

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:29.690
case. Because you have somebody who—

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.720
a similar leader but she throws

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:34.459
away her personality, her personal desires,

00:09:34.459 --> 00:09:37.339
her own individual wishes and everything

00:09:37.339 --> 00:09:39.649
out of deference to the long tradition.

00:09:39.649 --> 00:09:42.440
And in a way "The Crown" makes the case

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.139
that there's merit in that too because

00:09:45.139 --> 00:09:47.389
the disturbance itself can create an

00:09:47.389 --> 00:09:49.519
atmosphere in which bad things can

00:09:49.519 --> 00:09:51.949
happen. She wants to defer to the past.

00:09:51.949 --> 00:09:53.240
And in fact in the previous

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:55.069
generation you know they had the king

00:09:55.069 --> 00:09:57.350
that renounced his crown. [CROSSTALK] Edward.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.089
For the sake of love.

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:01.459
For the sake of love. And the monarchy was like

00:10:01.459 --> 00:10:03.079
don't ever do anything like that because

00:10:03.079 --> 00:10:04.790
that's not what the people need.

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.680
But that show makes the

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.680
case for

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.899
more of a continuity or a slow change. I think

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:13.540
we as libertarians are drawn to

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:14.980
both. Part of us—

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.320
part of us wants to disturb and upset

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.089
and overthrow. [CROSSTALK]  Part of this is

00:10:19.089 --> 00:10:20.680
also a little concerned about it that.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:22.510
This is the difference between a

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.460
kind of like Rothbard and Hayek perhaps.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:28.480
I think that the difference between Rothbard and Hayek is very interesting.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:29.500
You know I think all good

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.959
libertarians toggle between Hayek and Rothbard.

00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:33.550
Well we will stop it here, but what I

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.560
want to congratulate you on, because this—

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:38.290
you know is really revitalizing FEE

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:39.880
and I mean the material is good.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:42.130
This opens up a way of talking about

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.709
libertarianism as well as ideas as well

00:10:44.709 --> 00:10:46.450
as current popular culture in ways that

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:48.760
are just—it's very generative.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.350
Liberty is everything and it belongs to

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:52.480
everybody. And we should speak to

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.370
everybody I think. And not think of

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.040
ourselves as a small sect, but as Mises

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.720
said, "For the common good,

00:10:58.720 --> 00:10:59.380
for everybody."

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.060
We've been talking with Jeff Tucker. He

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:02.800
is the Director of Content at the

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.160
Foundation for Economic Education. FEE dot org.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.080
Jeff, thanks so much. It's my pleasure.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:23.799
For Reason TV, I'm Nick Gillespie.

